==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|la|en}} {{m|la|elevatus}}, past participle of {{m|la|elevare||to raise, lift up}}, from {{m|la|e||out}} + {{m|la|levare||to make light, to lift}}, from {{m|la|levis||light}}; see [[levity]] and [[lever]].

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˈɛləveɪt/|lang=en}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|elevat}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[raise]] (something) to a [[higher]] [[position]]; to [[lift]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[promote]] (someone) to a higher [[rank]].
#* '''2014''', A. D. Wright, ''The Early Modern Papacy''
#*: Much has also been made recently of the distorting effects exerted on the administration of Urban VIII by the interests of the Barberini nephews, especially of the two '''elevated''' to cardinal status.
#* '''2014''', Guy W. Lecky-Thompson, ''Inside SharePoint 2007 Administration'' (page 55)
#*: At that point, you have to '''elevate''' the account's rights, activate the feature, and then demote the account again.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[ennoble]] or [[honour]]/[[honor]] (someone).
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To lift someone's [[spirit]]s; to [[cheer up]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[increase]] the [[intensity]] of something, especially that of [[sound]].
#: ''to '''elevate''' the voice''
# {{lb|en|dated|colloquial|humorous}} To intoxicate in a slight degree; to render tipsy.
#* Sir Walter Scott
#*: The '''elevated''' cavaliers sent for two tubs of merry stingo.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|[[Latinism]]}} To lessen; to detract from; to disparage.
#: {{rfquotek|Jeremy Taylor}}

